selftext:
I've been working from a small two-character voice experiment and thinking about how its script would translate into pre-generated dialogue assets. This is an asset-authoring question; there isn't a game or engine integration behind it yet.

One character's turn contains “Don't,” a quieter “Please,” and then a longer sentence with a different direction. The spoken words, character voice and performance cues are separate things that could each change during revision.

A possible record for part of that turn would be:

    {
      "line_id": "goodbye_03",
      "speaker": "her",
      "segments": [
        {"text": "Don't.", "direction": "quick and sharp"},
        {"text": "Please.", "direction": "breath catches, voice breaks, very quiet"}
      ]
    }


The record is a proposed storage format, not an engine or model interface. The wording comes from an actual scripted test using Fish Audio Drama 3, whose weights are available under the Fish Audio Community License.

For people working with pre-generated VO: do you keep performance directions beside each line, in a separate take manifest, or in the dialogue tool itself? I'm particularly interested in how you track which audio needs replacing when the words stay the same but the acting direction changes.
